Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.760
Video is sponsored by Qiskit,&nbsp;
more details later in the video.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:10.960
From the first idea of a quantum computer in 1980&nbsp;
to today there has been huge growth in the quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:15.760
computing industry, especially in the last 10&nbsp;
years. With dozens of companies and startups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:20.880
spending hundreds of millions of dollars in a&nbsp;
race to build the world’s best quantum computers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:25.680
For most of us it’s quite hard to get our&nbsp;
heads around the world of quantum computing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:31.040
and a lot of information about it glosses over&nbsp;
important details. This video aims to clear all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.320
this up and if you watch the whole thing you’ll&nbsp;
have a very good overview of all the different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:39.760
kinds of quantum computing, how they work, and&nbsp;
why so many people are investing in the quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:46.560
computing industry. This is the map of quantum&nbsp;
computing. Quantum computers solve problems in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.200
a different way to the computers we are familiar&nbsp;
with, which, from now on I’ll be referring to as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:56.640
classical computers. Quantum computers have&nbsp;
certain advantages over normal computers for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.600
certain problems which comes from their ability&nbsp;
to be in a huge number of states at the same time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.800
whereas classical computers can only be in&nbsp;
one state at a time. To understand this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:12.880
and to understand how quantum computers work you&nbsp;
need to understand three things: superposition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:19.440
entanglement and interference. The building&nbsp;
blocks of a classical computer are called bits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:24.960
and the building blocks of a quantum computer are&nbsp;
called quantum bits, or qubits for short, and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:31.920
work in fundamentally different ways. A classical&nbsp;
bit is kind of like a switch that can either be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:37.360
a 0 or a 1 which you are probably already&nbsp;
familiar with as binary or binary information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:43.920
When we measure a bit we just get back the state&nbsp;
that it’s currently in, but we’ll see this isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:49.920
true of qubits. A qubit is more complicated. For&nbsp;
a useful visualisation you can think of them as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:55.600
an arrow pointing in 3D space. If they point&nbsp;
up they are in the 0 state and if they point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.640
down they are in the 1 state, just like a&nbsp;
classical bit, but they also have the option to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:05.280
be in a thing called a superposition state which&nbsp;
is when the arrow points in any other direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:13.680
This superposition state is a combination state of&nbsp;
0 and 1. Now, when you measure a qubit the output&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:20.480
it gives will still end up being either a 0 or a&nbsp;
1, but which one you get depends on a probability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:26.240
which is set by the direction of the arrow. If the&nbsp;
arrow is pointing more upwards you are more likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:32.800
to get back a 0 than a 1, and if it is pointing&nbsp;
downwards you are more likely to get a 1 than a 0,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:37.920
and if it is exactly on the equator&nbsp;
you’ll get either state with a 50%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:44.160
probability. So that’s the effect of superposition&nbsp;
explained, now we’ll move on to entanglement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:49.040
In a classical computer the bits&nbsp;
are independent from each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:53.040
The state of one bit is not influenced&nbsp;
by the state of any of the other bits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:57.520
But in quantum computers the qubits can be&nbsp;
entangled with each other which means they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:04.080
become part of one large quantum state together.&nbsp;
For an example let’s look at two qubits which are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:10.320
each in different superposition states, but aren’t&nbsp;
entangled yet. You can see the probabilities next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:14.240
to them, and these probabilities are&nbsp;
currently independent of each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:21.120
But when we entangle them, we have to throw away&nbsp;
those independent probabilities and calculate a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:29.360
probability distribution of all of the possible&nbsp;
states we can get out. Either 00, 01, 10, or 11.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:34.960
The important point here is because the qubits&nbsp;
are entangled, if you change the direction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:43.200
the arrow on one qubit, it changes the probability&nbsp;
distribution for the whole system, so the qubits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:50.480
are no longer independent of each other, they are&nbsp;
all part of the same large state. And this is true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:56.880
no matter how many qubits you have. You’ll also&nbsp;
note that for one qubit you have a probability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:03.040
distribution over 2 states. For two qubits you&nbsp;
have a probability distribution over 4 states.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:08.800
For three qubits you have a distribution over&nbsp;
8 states, and this keeps on doubling each time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:15.360
you add another qubit. In general, a quantum&nbsp;
computer of n qubits can be in a combination of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:23.120
2^n states. So I’d say this is the core difference&nbsp;
between classical computers and quantum computers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:29.360
Classical computers can be in any state you want,&nbsp;
but can only be in one state at a time, whereas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:34.800
quantum computers can be in a superposition&nbsp;
of all of those states at the same time. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:40.320
you may wonder how being in this superposition&nbsp;
state can be useful in a computer. Well for that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:45.760
we need the final component: interference.&nbsp;
To explain the effect of interference we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:50.560
need to go back and look at my picture of&nbsp;
a qubit technically called a Bloch sphere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:56.160
A qubit doesn’t actually look like this,&nbsp;
this is just a really nice way of visualising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:01.760
the state of a qubit. In reality the state of&nbsp;
a qubit is described by a more abstract entity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:07.520
known as a quantum wavefuncion. Wavefunctions&nbsp;
are the fundamental mathematical description&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:11.680
of everything in quantum mechanics which I’ve&nbsp;
described in more detail in a previous video.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:17.520
When you have many qubits entangled together all&nbsp;
of their wavefunctions are added together into an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:22.640
overall wavefunction describing the state&nbsp;
of the quantum computer. This adding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:28.080
together of wavefunctions is the interference&nbsp;
because, just like with say ripples of water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:33.280
when we add waves together they can constructively&nbsp;
interfere and add together to make a bigger wave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:38.960
or destructively interfere to cancel each&nbsp;
other out. The overall wavefunction of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:43.280
quantum computer is what sets the different&nbsp;
probabilities of the different states,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:48.160
and by changing the states of different qubits&nbsp;
we can change the probabilities that different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:53.600
states will come out when we measure the quantum&nbsp;
computer. Remember that even though the quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:58.640
computer can be in a superposition of millions&nbsp;
of states at the same time, when we measure it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:04.960
we only get a single state out. So when you are&nbsp;
using a quantum computer to solve a computation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:09.760
problem you need to use constructive interference&nbsp;
to increase the probability of the correct answer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:14.720
and use destructive interference to decrease&nbsp;
the probabilities of the incorrect answers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:20.880
so that when you measure it the correct answer&nbsp;
will come out. Now how you do this is the realm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:26.000
of quantum algorithms, and the whole motivation&nbsp;
behind quantum computing is that, theoretically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:30.400
there are a bunch of problems that you can solve&nbsp;
on a quantum computer that are thought to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:36.000
intractable on classical computers. Let’s take a&nbsp;
look at them. There are many quantum algorithms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:40.720
too many to describe in this video, so we’ll&nbsp;
just focus on the most famous and historically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:47.200
important: Shor’s algorithm. If you have two large&nbsp;
numbers and you multiply them together there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:52.880
a very fast, efficient, classical algorithm for&nbsp;
finding the answer. However, if you start with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:58.400
answer and ask, what are the original numbers that&nbsp;
multiply together to make this number? It is a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:03.520
more difficult. This is known as factorization,&nbsp;
and these numbers are called factors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:09.600
and the reason finding them is so hard is because&nbsp;
the search space of possible factors is so large.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:14.080
And there is no efficient classical algorithm&nbsp;
for finding the factors of large numbers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:20.480
For this reason we use this mathematical property&nbsp;
for internet encryption: secure websites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:26.320
emails and bank accounts. If you know these&nbsp;
factors you can easily decrypt the information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:31.600
but if you don’t you’d need to find them first&nbsp;
which is intractable on the world’s most powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:37.760
computers. Which is why in 1994, when Peter&nbsp;
Shor published a fast quantum algorithm that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:43.360
efficiently find the factors of large integers,&nbsp;
it caused quite the stir. This is the moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:47.840
that a lot of people started to take the idea&nbsp;
of quantum computing seriously because it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:54.160
the first application to a real world problem with&nbsp;
potentially huge real world security implications.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:00.560
But when I say a ‘fast’ quantum algorithm,&nbsp;
how fast, and how much faster than a classical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:05.120
computer would it be? To answer these questions&nbsp;
we need to take a little detour into the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:10.160
of quantum complexity theory. Quantum&nbsp;
complexity theory is a subfield of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:16.000
world of computational complexity theory which&nbsp;
deals with the categorisation of algorithms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:22.000
sorting them into bins according to how well they&nbsp;
run on computers. The categorisation is based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:28.480
how much harder it is to solve the problem as the&nbsp;
problem gets larger. Here any problem inside the P&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:34.160
box is easy to solve with a classical computer,&nbsp;
but anything outside it means we don’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:41.160
efficient classical algorithms to solve them and&nbsp;
factoring large numbers is one of these. But there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:47.360
is a box, BQP which is efficient for a quantum&nbsp;
computer, but not a classical computer. And these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:53.440
are the problems that quantum computers will be&nbsp;
better than classical computers at solving. As I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:58.640
said, complexity theory looks at how difficult it&nbsp;
is to solve a problem as the problem gets larger.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:04.640
So if you factorize a number with 8 digits, then&nbsp;
you add another digit on, how much harder is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:10.400
it to factor the new number compared to the old&nbsp;
one? Is it twice as hard? Exponentially harder?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:17.920
And what is the trend as you add more and more&nbsp;
digits? This is called its complexity or scaling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:20.640
and for factorisation it is exponential. Anything&nbsp;
with the N in the exponent is exponentially hard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:25.600
These exponential problems are the problems that&nbsp;
really screw you over as the problems get bigger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:30.240
and in the world of computer science you can win&nbsp;
respect and renown if you find a better algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:36.080
to solve these hardest problems. One example of&nbsp;
this was Shor’s algorithm which took advantage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:40.320
of the special features of quantum computers&nbsp;
to create an algorithm that could solve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:44.960
integer factorisation with a scaling much&nbsp;
better than the best classical algorithm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:50.880
The best classical algorithm is exponential,&nbsp;
whereas Shor’s algorithm is polynomial which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:55.760
is a huge deal in the world of complexity theory&nbsp;
and computer science in general because it turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.760 --> 00:10:02.320
an intractable problem into a problem that can&nbsp;
be solved. Solved, that is, if you have a working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:08.480
quantum computer, which is what people are working&nbsp;
on building. But you don’t need to worry about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:13.680
security of your bank account yet because today’s&nbsp;
quantum computers are not able to run Shor’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:19.840
algorithm on large numbers yet. I’ve estimated&nbsp;
they would need about a million qubits to do so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:25.840
but so far the most advanced universal quantum&nbsp;
computers have around 100. Also, people are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:31.120
working on what’s known as post-quantum encryption&nbsp;
schemes which don’t use integer factorization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:36.880
and another technology from the world of quantum&nbsp;
physics can help here too, a cryptographic scheme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:42.160
known as quantum cryptography. So that&nbsp;
was a look at just one quantum algorithm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:47.280
but there are many more each with different levels&nbsp;
of speedup. Another notable example is Grover’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:52.480
algorithm which can search unstructured lists of&nbsp;
data faster than the best classical algorithm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:58.000
But I should be careful here to make sure I&nbsp;
don’t mischaracterize classical computers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:02.800
They are very versatile devices, and there&nbsp;
is nothing to say that someone may find a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:08.240
very clever classical algorithm that could solve&nbsp;
the hardest problems like integer factorization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:12.960
more efficiently. People think it is&nbsp;
very unlikely, but it is not ruled out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:18.240
Also, there are problems that we can prove are&nbsp;
impossible to solve on classical computers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:23.840
called non-computable problems, like the halting&nbsp;
problem, but these are also impossible to solve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:28.480
on a quantum computer. So computationally&nbsp;
classical computers and quantum computers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:33.200
are equivalent to each other, the differences&nbsp;
all come from the algorithms that they can run.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:39.760
You can even simulate a quantum computer on a&nbsp;
classical computer and vice versa. But simulating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:44.496
a quantum computer on a classical computer gets&nbsp;
exponentially harder to do the more qubits you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.496 --> 00:11:49.920
are trying to simulate. This is because classical&nbsp;
computers struggle to simulate quantum systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:55.840
but because quantum computers are already quantum&nbsp;
systems, they don’t have this problem which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.840 --> 00:12:02.560
brings me to my favourite application of quantum&nbsp;
computers: quantum simulation. Quantum simulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:06.960
is simulating things like chemical reactions&nbsp;
or how electrons behave in different materials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:13.040
with a computer. Here quantum computers also have&nbsp;
an exponential speedup over classical computers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:18.480
because classical computers really struggle to&nbsp;
simulate quantum systems. Now I’ve made a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:23.280
other video about quantum simulation which you&nbsp;
can watch here, but basically simulating quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:29.280
systems with as few as 30 particles is difficult&nbsp;
even on the world’s most powerful supercomputers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:36.000
We also can’t do this on quantum computers yet,&nbsp;
but as they mature a main goal is to simulate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:42.160
larger and larger quantum systems. These include&nbsp;
areas like the behaviour of exotic materials at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:47.920
low temperatures like understanding what makes&nbsp;
some materials superconduct, or study important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:53.200
chemical reactions to improve their efficiency,&nbsp;
one example aims to produce fertiliser in a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:59.440
that emits way less carbon dioxide as fertiliser&nbsp;
production contributes to around 2% of global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:05.280
carbon emissions. Other potential applications&nbsp;
of quantum simulation include, improving solar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:11.520
panels, improving batteries, developing new&nbsp;
drugs, chemicals or materials for aerospace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:16.640
In general quantum simulation would mean that we&nbsp;
would be able to rapidly prototype many different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:22.480
materials inside a quantum computer and test all&nbsp;
their physical parameters, instead of having to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:28.160
physically make them and test them in a lab which&nbsp;
is a much more laborious and expensive process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.880 --> 00:13:34.960
This could be a lot faster and save a huge amount&nbsp;
of time and money. It is worth reiterating that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:38.960
these are all potential applications of quantum&nbsp;
computers, because we don’t have any quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:44.000
computers that can solve real world problems&nbsp;
better than our normal computers yet. But these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:49.840
are the kinds of problems quantum computers would&nbsp;
be well suited to. Other applications outside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:56.000
quantum simulation are optimization problems,&nbsp;
machine learning and A.I. Financial modelling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.000 --> 00:14:00.080
weather forecasting and climate change, which&nbsp;
I’ll be honest I don’t really understand how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.080 --> 00:14:05.680
this would work, and finally cybersecurity, which&nbsp;
I think just boils down to shor’s algorithm, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:12.640
I already described. Now we need to be a little&nbsp;
careful about the potential of hype here, as a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:18.160
of the claims of what quantum computers will be&nbsp;
good for come from people who are actively raising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:24.480
money to build them and so it makes sense for them&nbsp;
to piggyback on topical subjects in their pitches.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.360 --> 00:14:29.760
But my take on it is that historically, when a new&nbsp;
technology has come along, the people of the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:36.000
aren’t the best at being able to tell what it’s&nbsp;
going to be used for. For example the people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:43.760
invented the first computers never dreamed of the&nbsp;
internet, and all of the things on it. And this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:48.800
likely to be the same for quantum computers.&nbsp;
But for me the application that I can really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:53.840
understand the value of is quantum simulation&nbsp;
which is why I've focused on it in this video.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:59.600
Anyway, so far I’ve described how quantum&nbsp;
computers work and what problems they can solve.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:05.040
But most of what I’ve talked about so far is&nbsp;
theoretical. For the rest of the video I want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:09.920
to focus on reality. How are people actually&nbsp;
building quantum computers, and what can they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:15.520
actually do? Now it’s worth mentioning here&nbsp;
that some physicists are sceptical that it will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:21.040
ever be possible to build quantum computers at&nbsp;
the scale needed to solve real world problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:27.200
but people working on all of the following&nbsp;
certainly don’t agree. Now quantum computing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.200 --> 00:15:32.160
is often portrayed as if it is a single thing. But&nbsp;
inside the world of quantum computers there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:37.280
a large range of approaches to turning different&nbsp;
kinds of quantum systems into quantum computers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.280 --> 00:15:42.640
and there are two layers of nuance I need to talk&nbsp;
about. First of all are the models of quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:48.160
computing: the overall approach to manipulating a&nbsp;
collection of qubits and then there’s the physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:53.600
implementations: the actual quantum objects you&nbsp;
build your qubits from, like a superconducting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:58.960
loop, or individual atoms or photons. We’ll&nbsp;
start with the models of quantum computing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:04.020
It is interesting that there are different&nbsp;
models of quantum computing, because this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.020 --> 00:16:09.280
not something we see with classical computers.&nbsp;
Practically all the computers we use today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.280 --> 00:16:15.200
work in the same way, they have a bunch of bits&nbsp;
holding the binary information of ones and zeros,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:19.760
and we can do operations on these bits&nbsp;
using logical gates which are basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:26.320
simple operations that flip a bit, called a&nbsp;
NOT gate, or compare bits like giving you a 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.320 --> 00:16:32.400
if two bits are both zero, and a 0 if they&nbsp;
are anything else this is called a NOR gate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.400 --> 00:16:37.120
Interestingly you can build a full general&nbsp;
purpose computer from just bits and NOR gates.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:42.080
In quantum computing there is a similar&nbsp;
model called gate model, or circuit model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:47.440
which is the most popular and most&nbsp;
understood model of quantum computing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:52.880
In the circuit model you have your collection of&nbsp;
qubits which are entangled with each other, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.880 --> 00:16:58.240
then you have a bunch of gates which can perform&nbsp;
operations on small numbers of these qubits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:04.240
which change the states of the qubits without&nbsp;
measuring them. A quantum algorithm is built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.240 --> 00:17:10.480
from a sequence of gates applied to the qubits in&nbsp;
a certain order, and then a measurement at the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:14.800
when you get the final state, which hopefully is&nbsp;
the answer to the problem you are trying to solve.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:20.800
Simplistically you can think of these gates as&nbsp;
operations on the qubits that rotate the arrows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:25.520
to point in different directions. And these&nbsp;
operations change the probability of the final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:31.920
state of each qubit when it is finally measured.&nbsp;
Now there’s more to this which I don’t have time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:37.440
to explain here, but if you want to learn more&nbsp;
about them and do some quantum computing yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:41.600
I highly recommend the educational&nbsp;
website and YouTube channel called Qiskit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.560 --> 00:17:47.040
They are kindly sponsoring this video, and&nbsp;
honestly they are the best resource for people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:53.440
want to learn more about quantum computing and get&nbsp;
some actual hands-on experience. Basically Qiskit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.440 --> 00:17:58.560
is a software framework funded by IBM to make it&nbsp;
easier for people to get into the world of quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.560 --> 00:18:04.800
computing. Everything there is free to access and&nbsp;
the code is all open source, there is an online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:09.792
text book which teaches you all the basics, so if&nbsp;
you don’t have a quantum physics background that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.792 --> 00:18:14.480
is no problem at all, you can learn everything&nbsp;
you need there. Their Qiskit YouTube channel is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:18.880
also full of excellent tutorials and lectures,&nbsp;
I’ll link to all of this below. And in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.880 --> 00:18:24.560
quantum algorithms you can run through examples&nbsp;
of quantum circuits using their online tools.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.560 --> 00:18:28.400
And if you want to run your own quantum&nbsp;
programs you can download their open-source&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.400 --> 00:18:33.920
SDK and run them on IBM hardware, either on&nbsp;
classical simulators of quantum computers, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:42.000
on actual real world quantum computers, for free.&nbsp;
And the SDK is not only tied to IBM hardware. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:46.480
used to work at another quantum computing company&nbsp;
called D-Wave, and there is an interface to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.480 --> 00:18:52.080
computers in the SDK as well if you want to learn&nbsp;
about their approach called quantum annealing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.080 --> 00:18:56.960
many other companies are available too. Personally&nbsp;
I’ve been using their website to learn gate model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.960 --> 00:19:02.080
quantum computing deeper because my background is&nbsp;
in quantum annealing and I’m super happy that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:08.400
educational resource exists, and is free to use so&nbsp;
please check that out if you want to dig deeper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:12.560
Finally I just want to state that I’ve had&nbsp;
complete editorial control over the content&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.560 --> 00:19:18.480
of this video and my goal is always to be as&nbsp;
objective as I can, I just want to make sure you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.480 --> 00:19:23.360
know that Qiskit is funded by IBM who are building&nbsp;
quantum computers, and I used to work for D-Wave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.360 --> 00:19:28.480
who are making other quantum computers, just for&nbsp;
transparency so you know everyone’s backgrounds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:34.720
Right, back to the models of quantum computing&nbsp;
we’ve already looked at the circuit model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.720 --> 00:19:40.240
but closely related to it is measurement based or&nbsp;
one-way quantum computing which involves setting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.240 --> 00:19:46.560
up an initial entangled state, and then measuring&nbsp;
qubits one by one during the computation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:50.160
and mathematically this has been shown&nbsp;
to be equivalent to the circuit model.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.520 --> 00:19:55.520
Now let’s look at the models I’m most familiar&nbsp;
with: adiabatic quantum computing and quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.520 --> 00:20:00.640
annealing. Adiabatic quantum computing works&nbsp;
in a very different way to the circuit model.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.640 --> 00:20:05.520
In adiabatic quantum computing you are taking&nbsp;
advantage of one of the fundamental behaviours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.520 --> 00:20:10.720
in physics, the fact that every system in physics&nbsp;
always moves towards the minimum energy state.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:16.720
This is a very general principle, and adiabatic&nbsp;
quantum computing takes advantage of this by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.720 --> 00:20:21.600
posing the problems you want solved in such&nbsp;
a way so that the minimum energy state of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:27.520
quantum system is the answer to the problem.&nbsp;
You can picture this as an energy landscape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.520 --> 00:20:32.880
where each point on the landscape is one of the&nbsp;
potential outputs of the computer. In adiabatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:37.840
quantum computing you start off with a flat&nbsp;
landscape, and gradually introduce the energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.840 --> 00:20:42.720
landscape of your problem where the answer to&nbsp;
your problem is the lowest position on the map.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:46.560
If you do this slowly enough, the quantum&nbsp;
computer will always stay in the lowest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:51.040
energy state so that when you measure it you&nbsp;
are most likely to get the correct answer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:56.640
I should mention that I’m having to simplify&nbsp;
things a bit here to make it easier to understand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.640 --> 00:21:00.880
but it gives you the right picture of what is&nbsp;
going on. In reality I would need to talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.880 --> 00:21:07.280
Hamiltonian’s and eigenstates but that’s beyond&nbsp;
the scope of this video. Even though adiabatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.280 --> 00:21:11.680
quantum computing is so different to the circuit&nbsp;
model, they have been shown to be mathematically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.680 --> 00:21:16.720
equivalent, and problems can be mapped from one&nbsp;
to the other. And they are both something called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:22.560
a universal quantum computing scheme which means&nbsp;
that theoretically they can simulate any quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:28.400
system. Strongly related to adiabatic quantum&nbsp;
computing is quantum annealing which is not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.400 --> 00:21:33.760
universal quantum computing scheme, but works on&nbsp;
the same principle as adiabatic quantum computing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:38.320
with the system finding the minimum energy&nbsp;
state of an energy landscape that you give it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.760 --> 00:21:44.400
The reason it is not universal is because it&nbsp;
doesn’t have the full degrees of freedom to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:49.520
represent any quantum state, but even with this&nbsp;
limitation it can still be used to solve certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:49.520 --> 00:21:54.880
energy landscape problems like optimization&nbsp;
problems and simulate certain quantum systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.880 --> 00:21:59.360
and example is spin glasses which are grids&nbsp;
of magnetic fields connected to each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:04.880
And quantum annealing is a stepping stone to&nbsp;
building a universal adiabatic quantum computer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.240 --> 00:22:10.720
The last model we are going to look at is called&nbsp;
topological quantum computing which is currently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:10.720 --> 00:22:15.840
the most theoretical model of quantum computing&nbsp;
because it builds its qubits from an entity in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.840 --> 00:22:22.320
physics called a Majorana zero-mode quasi-particle&nbsp;
which is a type of non-abelian anyon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.680 --> 00:22:29.920
Which is a bit of a mouthful and obviously&nbsp;
quite confusing but the important term here is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.920 --> 00:22:36.560
quasi-particle. Quasi-particles aren’t fundamental&nbsp;
particles like atoms, electrons or photons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.560 --> 00:22:41.840
quasi-particles are created from the collected&nbsp;
behaviour of many particles together, and end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:48.240
having particle-like properties despite not being&nbsp;
actually real. The clearest example of this is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.240 --> 00:22:54.160
electron hole: if you have a grid of electrons&nbsp;
with a gap in the middle, as the electrons fill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:54.160 --> 00:22:59.440
in the gap it looks like this hole moves in&nbsp;
the opposite direction. This hole isn’t real,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:04.320
it’s just a hole, but you can treat it like&nbsp;
a particle with particle-like properties.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.280 --> 00:23:09.840
In condensed matter physics there are a large&nbsp;
range of different kinds of quasi-particle and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.840 --> 00:23:15.640
a Majorana zero-mode quasti-particle is an entity&nbsp;
that has been theoretically predicted, but there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.640 --> 00:23:20.320
is still significant debate over whether they’ve&nbsp;
actually been experimentally observed or not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:21.520 --> 00:23:26.080
Now the reason why physicists are excited about&nbsp;
this model is because these quasi-particles are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:26.080 --> 00:23:31.360
predicted to be a lot more stable than other&nbsp;
qubits because they are made from parts which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:31.360 --> 00:23:36.880
are physically separated from each other. This&nbsp;
is good because the main source of failure in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.880 --> 00:23:41.360
a quantum computer is noise, which comes from&nbsp;
rogue forms of energy creeping into the quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.360 --> 00:23:45.760
computer making the qubits drift away from&nbsp;
where they should be and causing errors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:51.840
But these quasi-particles are special because they&nbsp;
are protected from the noise by an energy gap.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:51.840 --> 00:23:57.040
Basically what this means is it takes a certain&nbsp;
energy to bring the parts of the Majorana particle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:57.040 --> 00:24:03.200
back together, so any perturbations of noise which&nbsp;
have a lower energy than that energy gap is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:09.360
felt by the quasi-particle. This might have been&nbsp;
a bit confusing, but that’s okay I’m still getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:13.520
my head around them too, but that was just the&nbsp;
best boiled down description I could come up with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:15.520 --> 00:24:20.240
Okay so that rounds up the different models of&nbsp;
quantum computation, but how do you actually build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:20.240 --> 00:24:25.120
them? There are a huge range of different physical&nbsp;
implementations of quantum computers because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:25.120 --> 00:24:29.440
there are so many different quantum systems&nbsp;
that you could potentially build them from.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.080 --> 00:24:35.680
The requirements to build a qubit is actually&nbsp;
fairly simple: all you need is a two state quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:35.680 --> 00:24:41.600
system when one state will represent 0 and the&nbsp;
other will represent 1. The most obvious example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:41.600 --> 00:24:46.880
of this is the spin of a particle: the spin can&nbsp;
be up or down, but as we shall see there are many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:52.640
properties of particles we can use. In fact, there&nbsp;
are too many for me to list them all, so I’m just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:52.640 --> 00:24:58.400
going to focus on the implementations that are the&nbsp;
most widely used and have been the most successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.400 --> 00:25:04.720
so far. But no matter what the approach is, they&nbsp;
all face a similar set of obstacles which we need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.720 --> 00:25:10.048
to take a look at first. In general it’s really&nbsp;
hard to control quantum systems because if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:10.048 --> 00:25:15.040
have got any slight interaction with the outside&nbsp;
world the information starts leaking away. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:15.040 --> 00:25:21.200
is called decoherence. You want your qubits to be&nbsp;
entangled with each other, but don’t want them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:21.200 --> 00:25:26.560
be entangled with anything else. But the trouble&nbsp;
is, your qubits will be made of physical stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:26.560 --> 00:25:30.080
and you will need other physical stuff&nbsp;
nearby to control and measure them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:30.640 --> 00:25:34.400
and your qubits are dumb they’ll&nbsp;
entangle with anything they can. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.400 --> 00:25:38.880
you need to design your qubits very carefully to&nbsp;
protect them from entangling with the environment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:39.760 --> 00:25:44.240
Then you need to shield your qubits from&nbsp;
any kind of noise: things like cosmic rays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:44.240 --> 00:25:50.080
or radiation from things like phone calls, or&nbsp;
heat energy or any other kind of rogue particle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:51.280 --> 00:25:56.640
Unfortunately some amount of decoherence and&nbsp;
noise is inevitable in any physical system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:56.640 --> 00:26:02.960
and is impossible to eliminate completely. And it&nbsp;
gets worse the more qubits you have entangled with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:02.960 --> 00:26:08.320
each other. This is the big open question still&nbsp;
hanging over the whole field of quantum computing:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:08.320 --> 00:26:13.680
is it ever possible to make a working quantum&nbsp;
computer with a large number of qubits, or will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:13.680 --> 00:26:20.240
decoherence and noise ruin everything? There&nbsp;
are strong opinions on both sides, and I guess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:20.240 --> 00:26:26.400
we won’t know for sure until we actually build&nbsp;
them. One plan to deal with decoherence and noise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:26.400 --> 00:26:31.360
is quantum error correction. This is an error&nbsp;
correction scheme to make fault-tolerant quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:31.360 --> 00:26:38.720
computers by using many entangled qubits together&nbsp;
to represent one noise free qubit. How many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:38.720 --> 00:26:45.520
you need depends on how good the qubits are, but&nbsp;
estimates are in the range of 100 to 1000 physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:45.520 --> 00:26:53.120
qubits to make one fault-tolerant qubit. Which&nbsp;
is a lot of qubits. And this brings us to another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:53.120 --> 00:26:59.440
major obstacle: scalability. For each qubit you&nbsp;
need to have a bunch of wires to manipulate and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:59.440 --> 00:27:04.320
measure it. For a small number of qubits this&nbsp;
is all manageable, but as the number of qubits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:04.320 --> 00:27:10.240
increases the amount of extra stuff you need&nbsp;
increases linearly, which is a massive engineering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:10.240 --> 00:27:15.680
problem. So any quantum computer design needs to&nbsp;
somehow be able to entangle all of the qubits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:15.680 --> 00:27:22.320
and then control and measure them in a scalable&nbsp;
way. So those are all the problems with building a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:22.320 --> 00:27:28.160
real quantum computer, let’s take a look at the&nbsp;
different approaches scientists are pursuing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:34.800
Superconducting quantum computers are currently&nbsp;
the most popular approach. A superconducting qubit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:34.800 --> 00:27:40.240
is made from superconducting wires with a break&nbsp;
in the superconductor called a josephson junction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:41.280 --> 00:27:45.920
The most popular type of superconducting&nbsp;
qubit is called a transmon where the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:45.920 --> 00:27:51.360
level system is encoded in pairs of the&nbsp;
electric charge moving across the junction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:51.360 --> 00:27:56.400
specifically the frequency at which charges&nbsp;
oscillate back and forth across the junction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:56.400 --> 00:28:00.400
But there have been other designs that&nbsp;
use the magnetic flux in a loop of wire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:00.400 --> 00:28:06.160
or the phase across a wire as a two level&nbsp;
system known as flux qubits or phase qubits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:07.680 --> 00:28:12.640
Physicists have also looked at ways of making&nbsp;
qubits out of fundamental entities like atoms, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:12.640 --> 00:28:18.320
electrons or photons. Next are quantum dot quantum&nbsp;
computers or silicon spin quantum computers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:18.880 --> 00:28:23.920
Here I’m using quantum dot quantum computers&nbsp;
to collect a range of qubit designs built from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:23.920 --> 00:28:29.040
semiconductors, things like silicon. Here the&nbsp;
qubits are made from electrons or even groups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:29.040 --> 00:28:35.920
of electrons and the two level system is encoded&nbsp;
into the spin or charge of the electrons. On the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:35.920 --> 00:28:40.720
chip there’s a small area where the electron&nbsp;
is restricted to is called a quantum dot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:41.280 --> 00:28:46.080
and operations on the qubits are performed through&nbsp;
voltages on the chip, or microwaves or magnetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:46.080 --> 00:28:51.280
fields. As well as silicon, people have also&nbsp;
used other semiconducting materials like gallium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:51.280 --> 00:28:57.840
arsenide, silicon carbide and also diamond amongst&nbsp;
others, which all have different properties.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:58.960 --> 00:29:04.720
Next we have linear optical quantum computing.&nbsp;
Optical quantum computers use photons of light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:04.720 --> 00:29:09.360
as the qubits and they operate on these&nbsp;
qubits using optical elements like mirrors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.360 --> 00:29:15.280
waveplates and interferometers. At scale this has&nbsp;
been accomplished by printing these elements into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:15.280 --> 00:29:22.560
integrated photonics chips. The two level system&nbsp;
in an optical quantum computer can have different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:22.560 --> 00:29:28.800
designs, either a superposition of different&nbsp;
paths a single photon takes through the chip,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:28.800 --> 00:29:35.280
or a superposition of different numbers of photons&nbsp;
present in a path. And these can be manipulated by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:35.280 --> 00:29:42.640
applying a voltage to a path. Now onto trapped&nbsp;
ion quantum computers which use charged atoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:42.640 --> 00:29:48.640
as qubits. These atoms are ionised, having a&nbsp;
missing electron, which makes them electrically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:48.640 --> 00:29:54.240
charged and means they can be levitated and&nbsp;
moved about with electromagnetic fields.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:54.240 --> 00:29:59.840
Here the two level state that encodes the qubit&nbsp;
are two specific energy levels of the atom which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:59.840 --> 00:30:05.680
can be manipulated or measured with microwaves&nbsp;
or laser beams. Next we have colour centre or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:05.680 --> 00:30:10.480
nitrogen vacancy quantum computers which are&nbsp;
similar to trapped ion quantum computers in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:10.480 --> 00:30:15.760
that the qubits are made from atoms, but instead&nbsp;
of being trapped in an electromagnetic field,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:15.760 --> 00:30:21.840
they are embedded in a gap of the material like&nbsp;
nitrogen embedded in diamond or silicon carbide.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:22.720 --> 00:30:25.840
There are a few different ways to make&nbsp;
these, but typically the qubits are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:25.840 --> 00:30:30.960
the nuclear spins of the embedded atoms and&nbsp;
they are entangled together with electrons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:32.480 --> 00:30:37.600
The final approach is called neutral atoms&nbsp;
in optical lattices. In this approach the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:37.600 --> 00:30:44.080
qubits are atoms, and the design uses cold atom&nbsp;
physics capturing neutral atoms like caesium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:44.080 --> 00:30:49.520
into an optical lattice which is a crisscrossed&nbsp;
arrangement of laser beams, which form energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:49.520 --> 00:30:55.360
wells shaped kind of like an egg box. These atoms&nbsp;
are cooled down with lasers to a few millionths of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:55.360 --> 00:31:00.160
a kelvin and there are a number of ways to encode&nbsp;
the two level system the qubit is built from:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:00.160 --> 00:31:05.280
either the hyperfine energy level of the atom&nbsp;
or excited states and they can also make use of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:05.280 --> 00:31:10.560
Rydberg atoms. And the atoms can be controlled&nbsp;
and entangled with each other with lasers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:11.840 --> 00:31:17.440
They can also be used as quantum simulators&nbsp;
as well as quantum computers. In fact a 10,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:17.440 --> 00:31:22.640
atom quantum simulator has been made, but this&nbsp;
doesn’t look like a universal quantum computer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:23.760 --> 00:31:28.640
These are the main approaches I’m going to cover&nbsp;
in this video, but it is not an exhaustive list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:28.640 --> 00:31:32.480
some other qubit designs include:&nbsp;
Electron-on-helium qubit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:32.480 --> 00:31:40.560
Cavity quantum electrodynamics, Magnetic Molecule,&nbsp;
Molecular Spins, NMR quantum computers. But these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:40.560 --> 00:31:44.560
have not been built at the same scale as the&nbsp;
other approaches I mentioned in more detail.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:45.440 --> 00:31:49.600
So that was the map of quantum computing and&nbsp;
that should give you an excellent overview of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:49.600 --> 00:31:53.920
field. As you can see, there are many different&nbsp;
approaches to building a quantum computer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:53.920 --> 00:31:58.640
and what is so interesting is that it’s not yet&nbsp;
